* @param $write The sockets listed in the `$write` array will be watched to see if a write will not block.
* @param $except The sockets listed in the `$except` array will be watched for exceptions.
* @param $seconds The `$seconds` and `$microseconds` together form the `timeout` parameter. The `timeout` is an upper bound on the amount of time elapsed before `socket_select()` return. `$seconds` may be zero , causing `socket_select()` to return immediately. This is useful for polling. If `$seconds` is **`null`** (no timeout), `socket_select()` can block indefinitely.
* @param $microseconds
* @return On success `socket_select()` returns the number of sockets contained in the modified arrays, which may be zero if the timeout expires before anything interesting happens.On error **`false`** is returned. The error code can be retrieved with `socket_last_error()`.
* @link https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.socket-select.php
* @generated from PHP v8.5.8, EXT: sockets v8.5.8
*/
